<!DOCTYPE html>
<!-- Generated by the goat-get Ansible role — do not edit on host. -->
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>goatbuilder download · {{ goat_get_hostname }}</title>
  <style>
    :root { color-scheme: dark; }
    body { font: 15px/1.6 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
           max-width: 46rem; margin: 3rem auto; padding: 0 1.2rem;
           background: #14110d; color: #e7dcc8; }
    h1 { font-size: 1.4rem; color: #e8b04b; }
    h2 { font-size: 1rem; color: #c9a35a; margin-top: 2rem; }
    a { color: #e8b04b; }
    code, pre { background: #211b13; }
    pre { padding: .9rem 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto;
          border: 1px solid #3a2f1f; }
    small { color: #9a8c70; }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; }
    td, th { text-align: left; padding: .3rem 1rem .3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #3a2f1f; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>goatbuilder — {{ goat_get_goatbuilder_release_tag }}</h1>
  <p>The build/release command-line for a goat environment: declare a release,
     converge the environment, verify every machine from its own record. For
     platform admins who deploy and release; not the <code>goat</code> client
     (that is <a href="/">one level up</a>).</p>

  <h2>Verify first</h2>
  <p>One signature covers the release: <code>SHA256SUMS</code> lists every
     artifact and is signed by the release workflow, so verifying it and then
     checking hashes against it covers the binary you download. The signature
     is made at release time, not here — this surface is a mirror, not a
     signer, and a copy from GitHub verifies identically.</p>
  <pre>curl -fLO https://{{ goat_get_hostname }}/{{ goat_get_goatbuilder_www_subdir }}/SHA256SUMS
curl -fLO https://{{ goat_get_hostname }}/{{ goat_get_goatbuilder_www_subdir }}/SHA256SUMS.cosign-bundle
cosign verify-blob \
  --bundle SHA256SUMS.cosign-bundle \
  --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github.com/{{ goat_get_goatbuilder_release_repo }}/' \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  SHA256SUMS</pre>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <pre>OS=$(uname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
ARCH=$(uname -m | sed 's/x86_64/amd64/;s/aarch64/arm64/')
curl -fLO https://{{ goat_get_hostname }}/{{ goat_get_goatbuilder_www_subdir }}/goatbuilder-$OS-$ARCH
sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS        # shasum -a 256 -c on macOS
install -m 0755 goatbuilder-$OS-$ARCH ~/.local/bin/goatbuilder
goatbuilder --version</pre>
  <p><small>macOS: if the release log says the binary was ad-hoc signed rather
     than notarized, run <code>codesign --force --sign - ~/.local/bin/goatbuilder</code>
     after the checksum passes; a linker-signed binary can hang at launch.</small></p>

  <h2>Then</h2>
  <pre>cd &lt;your checkout of the source repository, on main&gt;
goatbuilder doctor                    # what a run needs, before anything is cut
goatbuilder deploy --env &lt;env&gt; --component &lt;c&gt; --dry-run</pre>
  <p>The first-run guide, the design and the contract are in the book at
     <a href="https://docs.{{ site_id }}.{{ site_dns_zone }}/goatbuilder/">docs.{{ site_id }}.{{ site_dns_zone }}/goatbuilder/</a>.</p>

  <h2>Artifacts</h2>
  <table>
  <tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
{% for asset in goat_get_goatbuilder_assets %}
  <tr><td><a href="{{ asset }}">{{ asset }}</a></td>
  <td>{% if asset.endswith('.sha256') %}single-artifact checksum{% elif asset == 'SHA256SUMS.cosign-bundle' %}signature over SHA256SUMS{% elif asset == 'SHA256SUMS' %}checksums for every artifact{% elif asset.endswith('.tar.gz') %}the docs book, as served at docs.{{ site_id }}.{{ site_dns_zone }}/goatbuilder/{% elif 'linux' in asset %}Linux binary (glibc 2.31 floor){% elif 'darwin' in asset %}macOS binary{% else %}—{% endif %}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
  </table>
  <p><a href="/">← back to the goat download surface</a></p>
</body>
</html>
